# Design: Hold one read snapshot for a stepped copy

## Problem
store.Backup (the recovery point before a migration) and
store.FTSIndexConsistent (ohya doctor --deep) copied the hub database
with sqlite3_backup_step in a loop and held no read transaction on the
source between steps. SQLite opens a read transaction on the source
only when a step finds none open, and closes it at the end of that
step. A commit from another connection between two steps then makes
the next step restart the copy at page 1 (sqlite3BackupRestart through
pager_reset). On a hub that is written continuously, the copy can run
until its deadline without finishing.

## Approach
copySnapshot in internal/store/backup.go begins a deferred transaction
on the source connection and runs one real read, so that SQLite opens
its read snapshot before the first step. It holds the transaction until
the last step ends, then rolls back. Both copy loops call it.

## Key Trade-offs
- The hub always runs in WAL mode, so a held read transaction does not
  block writers. The WAL grows by the writes made during the copy. In
  rollback-journal mode the copy would block writers; verifyReader
  refuses that mode.
- go-sqlite3 v1.14.53 has no SessionResetter. When the rollback fails,
  copySnapshot returns driver.ErrBadConn, so database/sql discards the
  connection instead of returning it to the pool with an open
  transaction.
- The copy has no rate limit. It yields 1 ms after each 128 pages.

## Evidence
- Two regression tests hold a second connection that commits during
  the copy. Before the fix: 1,377 steps and then the 30 s deadline
  (Backup); 41,658 commits and then the deadline (FTSIndexConsistent).
  After the fix: 0.23 s and 0.64 s.
- Live hub, 2026-11-24: ohya doctor --deep passed in 24 min 45 s with
  this commit while the hub services ran.
- The recovery copy of 8.5 h on 2026-11-16 fits many restarts and one
  last pass after the other writers stopped. That is an inference; no
  log shows the restarts.

